An API key is a simple authentication mechanism where the client sends a predefined secret with each request. OAuth2/JWT provides a more complete authentication and authorization framework, including user identity, token expiration, scopes, and fine-grained permissions. In production, I would typically use OAuth2/OIDC with short-lived JWT access tokens, HTTPS, token rotation, proper secret management, rate limiting, and role-based authorization instead of relying on one static API key.